      <h1>Generate production-ready PDF invoices in ASP.NET Core</h1>
      <p class="article-deck">Build a real invoice endpoint with deterministic HTML, page-break control, repeating headers, culture-aware currency, and a render queue that survives traffic spikes.</p>
      <div class="author-line"><span class="avatar large">AP</span><div><b>Abhishek Panda</b><small>AI Solution Architect · Aug 30, 2026 · 29 min read</small></div><button>Follow</button></div>
      <div class="article-hero-code"><div class="window-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>InvoiceEndpoint.cs</span></div><pre><code><span class="violet">app</span>.MapPost(<span class="green">"/invoices/&#123;id:guid&#125;/pdf"</span>, <span class="violet">async</span> (
    Guid id, IInvoicePdfQueue queue, CancellationToken ct) =&gt;
&#123;
    <span class="violet">var</span> job = <span class="violet">await</span> queue.EnqueueAsync(id, ct);
    <span class="violet">return</span> Results.Accepted(<span class="green">$"/pdf-jobs/&#123;job.Id&#125;"</span>, job);
&#125;);</code></pre></div>
      <div class="learning-box"><strong>What you’ll build</strong><p>A queued rendering pipeline that separates the request path from browser work, returns traceable job IDs, and produces consistent invoice files.</p></div>
      <section id="why"><span class="section-number">01</span><h2>Why PDF generation becomes an architecture problem</h2><p>Rendering one invoice is easy. Rendering thousands safely is where the design earns its keep. A browser process consumes memory, external assets can stall, and retries may create duplicate work.</p><p>The endpoint should validate intent and enqueue work. A bounded worker owns rendering. Storage owns the finished artifact. That separation protects API latency while keeping failure visible.</p><blockquote><strong>Design rule</strong><p>Treat document rendering as a workload, not a formatting helper.</p></blockquote></section>
      <section id="pipeline"><span class="section-number">02</span><h2>Shape the rendering pipeline</h2><div class="pipeline"><span>API request</span><b>→</b><span>Bounded queue</span><b>→</b><span>HTML renderer</span><b>→</b><span>Object storage</span></div><p>The job record is the spine of this flow. It carries status, attempt count, output location, failure reason, and a correlation ID. Clients poll the status endpoint or receive a webhook when processing finishes.</p>
      <div class="code-block"><div class="code-title"><span>InvoicePdfJob.cs</span><button>Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="violet">public sealed record</span> InvoicePdfJob(
    Guid Id,
    Guid InvoiceId,
    PdfJobStatus Status,
    <span class="blue">int</span> Attempt,
    Uri? DownloadUrl,
    <span class="blue">string</span>? FailureReason);</code></pre></div></section>
      <section id="css"><span class="section-number">03</span><h2>Make print CSS deterministic</h2><p>Invoice HTML is a versioned contract. Pin fonts, inline critical assets, declare page size, and test long tables rather than hoping the browser makes tasteful decisions.</p>
      <div class="code-block"><div class="code-title"><span>invoice.css</span><button>Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="violet">@page</span> &#123; size: A4; margin: 18mm 14mm; &#125;
thead &#123; display: table-header-group; &#125;
tr &#123; break-inside: avoid; &#125;
.totals &#123; break-inside: avoid; margin-top: 24px; &#125;</code></pre></div></section>
      <section id="recap"><span class="section-number">04</span><h2>Production checklist</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>Bound concurrency and enforce a render timeout.</li><li>Sanitize untrusted HTML and block arbitrary network access.</li><li>Make jobs idempotent with a stable invoice-version key.</li><li>Record render duration, failures, queue depth, and file size.</li><li>Expire temporary files with a storage lifecycle policy.</li></ul></section>
